<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Environment and Recycling – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Environment and Recycling</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> In the Past, People Didn't Recycle as Much as They Do Now.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Explain abstract concepts, using simple analogies and native language when necessary. Concept: Interpret graphical data for class discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain the concept of recycling, using simple analogies, gestures, and brief phrases in the native language when necessary (e.g., showing circular hand motions to represent recycling as a "cycle" and saying "recycle" to reinforce understanding).
            Can interpret recycling data from visual aids, such as charts or graphs, by explaining key sections (e.g., pointing to a pie chart segment and saying, "This part shows how much plastic we recycle compared to paper.")
            Can use visual aids, such as flow charts, diagrams, and gestures to clarify abstract environmental concepts (e.g., using a flow chart to show the steps in the recycling process and gestures to emphasize the "cycle" of reuse).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays two sets of pictures: one showing an old landfill or people throwing trash everywhere ("In the past...") and another showing modern recycling bins or people actively recycling ("Today..."). Students work in pairs to describe what they see, using simple sentences to contrast the past and present. The teacher asks, "What do you see in the old pictures? What about the new ones?" to activate prior knowledge on environmental practices.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to explain a simple environmental concept like "reduce" using gestures and an analogy. For example, by holding up a plastic bottle and saying, "To <b>reduce</b> means to use less of something, like buying fewer plastic bottles. If I stop buying this, I <b>reduce</b> plastic <b>waste</b>." (gestures shrinking the bottle). The teacher then models explaining the pronunciation of "-ed" endings for words like "saved" /d/ and "watched" /t/, contrasting them with their present forms.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I stop using plastic bags, am I reducing or increasing waste?" (Expected answer: Reducing.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'reduce' about using more or less of something?" (Expected answer: Less.)</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher reinforces the contrast between "In the past, people didn't recycle that much" and "Today people are more aware."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Environment and Recycling".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a simple infographic or a bar chart showing "Recycling Rates: Past vs. Present in Our Community" or a flow chart illustrating "The Steps of Recycling Plastic." This graphical data could show, for example, a low percentage for "plastic recycled in 2000" and a higher percentage for "plastic recycled today."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret graphical data for class discussions and explain abstract concepts using simple analogies and gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> The teacher asks: "What is this chart about?" and "What is the main difference between the past and today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students identify and point to key sections of the chart (e.g., "Which bar shows plastic recycling in the past? Which shows today?"). They identify words like 'waste', 'recycle', 'pollution' if visible in the infographic.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher highlights grammatical structures like "In the past, people <b>didn't recycle</b> much plastic" and "Today, we <b>recycle</b> more."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice explaining concepts and data, using Past simple vs. present practices, First conditional, Modal 'should', and vocabulary (recycle, waste, environment, pollution, energy, landfill, planet, resource, reduce, reuse, clean, save, protect, environmental, clean, renewable, important). Pronunciation focus on stress contrasts and "-ed" endings: /d/ in "saved," /ɪd/ in "wanted," and /t/ in "watched."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - Sentence Mediation:</b> Students complete sentences that contrast past and present environmental actions or explain cause-effect. For example: "In the past, people ____ (not reuse) plastic bags. Today, many people ____ (reuse) them." or "If we ____ (waste) water, there ____ (be) less for everyone." They also practice identifying and correcting "should" statements about giving advice. The teacher monitors pronunciation of verb endings, specifically "-ed" sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled - Interpret and Explain:</b> In pairs, students are given simplified diagrams or flow charts (e.g., "How a plastic bottle is recycled" or "Water Cycle"). They take turns explaining the process or data to their partner, using gestures and target vocabulary. Sentence starters are provided: "This diagram shows...", "First, we...", "If we do this, then...", "You should...". The teacher circulates, offering support and checking for appropriate use of gestures and analogies.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - Concept Clarification:</b> Students write down two environmental words from the lesson (e.g., "pollution," "renewable energy," "landfill"). They exchange words with a partner. Each student must explain their new word to their partner without using that exact word, employing simple analogies, gestures, or drawing a quick sketch. The partner tries to guess the word. The teacher encourages the use of contrasting past/present examples where possible (e.g., "In the past, there was a lot of [pollution] from factories.").</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Environment and Recycling".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Eco-Friendly Tutorial Explanations:</b> Students, working in their existing groups for the "Eco-Friendly Tutorials" project, prepare and present a 2-3 minute segment of their tutorial to the class. Their presentation must focus on mediating information about an eco-friendly practice.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group selects one key eco-friendly practice from their tutorial (e.g., "How to compost," "Why we should reduce plastic," "The benefits of saving water").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must explain this concept or process clearly to the class, using any visual aids (pictures, drawings, simple flow charts) they have for their tutorial.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They are required to use simple analogies, gestures, and contrasting statements: "In the past, people <b>didn't recycle</b> much. Now, we <b>should recycle</b> everything we can." or "If we <b>save</b> water, we <b>protect</b> our <b>planet</b>."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students should actively use vocabulary such as `recycle, reduce, reuse, save, protect, waste, environment, pollution, energy`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages brief phrases in the native language if necessary to clarify complex terms, immediately followed by the English equivalent and a gesture (e.g., showing a circular motion for "cycle" and saying "reciclar - recycling is a cycle!").</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Teacher Observation Checklist.</b> The teacher uses a simple checklist to observe each group's presentation during Stage 4. Criteria include:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Explains concepts clearly using analogies/gestures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Interprets visuals effectively (if used).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses past simple vs. present tense for contrast.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Includes 'should' for advice and 'if...then' for cause-effect.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., recycle, waste, protect).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of '-ed' endings is mostly intelligible.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborates effectively within the group.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their chosen segment of the "Eco-Friendly Tutorial," demonstrating their ability to mediate information about eco-friendly practices, explain abstract concepts like "resource depletion" using simple analogies and gestures, and interpret visual data (if their tutorial includes charts/graphs) to contrast past and present actions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b> Intelligible pronunciation of key words and verb endings (-ed), accurate use of target vocabulary, clear and coherent explanation of concepts, effective use of gestures and visual aids, appropriate use of past vs. present contrasts and modal 'should', and collaborative effort in presentation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences before leaving:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I learned how to explain _______ using ______ and ______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I still find a little difficult to explain is _______."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effective were my gestures and analogies in helping my classmates understand our eco-friendly tutorial?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What could I do differently next time to explain a concept more clearly or interpret data better?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in teaching mediation skills, especially with the Eco-Friendly Tutorials? Students effectively used gestures and analogies, and the contrast between past and present practices was clearly articulated by many. The opportunity to use brief native language phrases helped build confidence. What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for next time? Some students struggled with interpreting more complex graphical data; more explicit practice on breaking down charts into simple explanations could be beneficial. I need to ensure consistent checking for "-ed" ending pronunciation. The collaborative aspect was strong, showing effective sociolinguistic competence.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the unit on "The Environment and Recycling." The next unit will build on these communication skills by applying them to a new thematic area, requiring students to continue using mediation skills to explain new concepts and synthesize information for different communicative tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>